<div class="hebrew-block">וְעַל-כֵּן צְרִיכִין לִלְמֹד עִם בְּנוֹ וְעִם בֶּן בְּנוֹ, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב, וְהוֹדַעְתָּם לְבָנֶיךָ וְלִבְנֵי בָנֶיךָ וְכוּ' וְלֹא עִמָּהֶם לְבַד וְכוּ'. אֶלָּא שֶׁחַיָּבִין לְהַקְדִּים אוֹתָם וְכוּ'. כִּי הַתּוֹרָה, שֶׁהִיא בְּחִינַת אוֹר הַנְּקֻדָּה, צְרִיכִין לְקַבֵּל בְּשָׁלֹשׁ בְּחִינוֹת, דְּהַיְנוּ א. לְדַבֵּר עִם הַצַּדִּיק לְקַבֵּל מִמֶּנּוּ נְקֻדָּה הַכְּלָלִיּוּת, ב. לְדַבֵּר עִם חֲבֵרוֹ בִּבְחִינַת וּמְקַבְּלִין דֵּין מִן דֵּין, ג. לְדַבֵּר בֵּינוֹ לְבֵין קוֹנוֹ לְקַבֵּל הַנְּקֻדָּה מִנֵּהּ וּבֵהּ כַּנַּ"ל. וְזֶה יָדוּעַ שֶׁבַּקְּדֻשָּׁה כָּל בְּחִינָה וּבְחִינָה צְרִיכָה לִהְיוֹת כָּלוּל מִכָּל הַבְּחִינוֹת. וְעַל-כֵּן הַנְּקֻדָּה שֶׁל כָּל אֶחָד מִיִּשְׂרָאֵל בְּעַצְמוֹ צְרִיכָה לְהָאִיר בְּשָׁלֹשׁ בְּחִינוֹת הַנַּ"ל. וְזֶה בְּחִינַת וְהוֹדַעְתָּם לְבָנֶיךָ וְלִבְנֵי בָנֶיךָ כְּנֶגֶד שָׁלֹשׁ נְקֻדּוֹת הַנַּ"ל, כִּי כְּשֶׁלּוֹמֵד בְּעַצְמוֹ וּמְדַבֵּר דִּבְרֵי הַתּוֹרָה בְּפִיו וּמְקַשְּׁרָם לְלִבּוֹ אֲזַי מֵאִיר הַנְּקֻדָּה מִפִּיו לְלִבּוֹ מִנֵּהּ וּבֵהּ וּכְשֶׁזּוֹכֶה לְהוֹלִיד בֵּן מְחֻיָּב לִלְמֹד עִמּוֹ. וְאָז טוֹבִים הַשְּׁנַיִם מִן הָאֶחָד שֶׁאִם יִפֹּלוּ הָאֶחָד יָקִים אֶת חֲבֵרוֹ, כִּי אָז מְאִירָה הַנְּקֻדָּה הַשְּׁנִיָּה שֶׁמֵּאִיר כָּל אֶחָד מִיִּשְׂרָאֵל לַחֲבֵרוֹ בִּבְחִינַת וּמְקַבְּלִין דֵּין מִן דֵּין, כִּי גַּם הַבָּנִים נִקְרָאִים אַחִים וַחֲבֵרִים, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב, וַיֹּאמֶר יַעֲקֹב לְאֶחָיו לִקְטוּ אֲבָנִים. וּפֵרֵשׁ רַשִׁ"י, לְאֶחָיו לְבָנָיו שֶׁהָיוּ אַחִים נִגָּשִׁים אֵלָיו וְכוּ'. וּכְשֶׁזּוֹכֶה לְבֶן בְּנוֹ מְחֻיָּב לִלְמֹד עִמּוֹ. וְאָז הַחוּט הַמְשֻׁלָּשׁ לֹא בִּמְהֵרָה יִנָּתֵק, כִּי אָז צָרִיךְ לִלְמֹד עִם בְּנוֹ וְעִם בֶּן בְּנוֹ וְאָז נִתְגַּדְּלָה נְקֻדָּתוֹ וְנַעֲשֵׂית בִּבְחִינַת אוֹר נְקֻדַּת הָרַב. מֵאַחַר שֶׁזּוֹכֶה לִלְמֹד עִם שְׁנַיִם עִם בְּנוֹ וְעִם בֶּן בְּנוֹ, כִּי אָז בְּנוֹ בְּעַצְמוֹ לוֹמֵד עִם בְּנוֹ שֶׁהוּא בֶּן בְּנוֹ. וְלִמּוּדָם שֶׁלּוֹמְדִים יַחַד הוּא בְּחִינַת אוֹר הַנְּקֻדָּה שֶׁל הַחֲבֵרִים כַּנַּ"ל וְהוּא נַעֲשֶׂה זָקֵן וְלוֹמֵד עִם שְׁנֵיהֶם. עַל-כֵּן נְקֻדָּתוֹ בִּבְחִינַת נְקֻדַּת הָרַב שֶׁנִּקְרָא זָקֵן זֶה קָנָה חָכְמָה, כִּי הוּא מֵאִיר בִּשְׁנֵיהֶם בִּבְנוֹ וּבֶן בְּנוֹ בִּבְחִינַת נְקֻדַּת הָרַב שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת זָקֵן. וּבְנוֹ וּבֶן בְּנוֹ מְאִירִין זֶה בָּזֶה בִּבְחִינַת נְקֻדַּת הַחֲבֵרִים. וְכָל אֶחָד לוֹמֵד בְּעַצְמוֹ וּמֵאִיר הַנְּקֻדָּה מִנֵּהּ וּבֵהּ. וַאֲזַי מְאִירִין כָּל הַשָּׁלֹשׁ נְקֻדּוֹת וְנִתְבַּטְּלִין כָּל הַחֲרָפוֹת וְהַשְּׁבִירוֹת כַּנַּ"ל, כִּי הַשָּׁלֹשׁ נְקֻדּוֹת הַנַּ"ל הֵם בְּחִינַת שְׁלֹשָׁה אָבוֹת אַבְרָהָם, יִצְחָק וְיַעֲקֹב. בִּתְחִלָּה הָיְתָה נְקֻדַּת אַבְרָהָם רַק מִנֵּהּ וּבֵהּ, כִּי הוּא הָיָה הָרִאשׁוֹן שֶׁהִכִּיר אֶת בּוֹרְאוֹ בִּשְׁלֵמוּת וְהִתְחִיל לְהָאִיר אוֹר הַנְּקֻדָּה בָּעוֹלָם לְבַטֵּל הַחֲרָפוֹת וְהַשְּׁבִירוֹת שֶׁהִתְגַּבְּרוּ מֵחֵטְא אָדָם הָרִאשׁוֹן, כִּי אַבְרָהָם זָכָה לִכְהֻנָּה, בְּחִינַת אַהֲבָה הַקְּדוֹשָׁה, בְּחִינַת אַתָּה כֹהֵן לְעוֹלָם. וְזֶה זָכָה עַל-יְדֵי שֶׁהִשְׁתַּדֵּל כָּל יָמָיו בְּהִתְבּוֹדְדוּת לְדַבֵּר בֵּינוֹ לְבֵין קוֹנוֹ וּלְדַבֵּר עִם בְּנֵי אָדָם שֶׁעַל-יְדֵי-זֶה זָכָה לְהָאִיר אוֹר הַנְּקֻדָּה. וְעַל-יְדֵי-זֶה זָכָה לָאַהֲבָה הַקְּדוֹשָׁה שֶׁשּׁוֹרָה אֵצֶל הַנְּקֻדָּה בְּחִינַת אַבְרָהָם אוֹהֲבִי כַּנַּ"ל. אֲבָל בִּתְחִלָּה הָיְתָה נְקֻדַּת אַבְרָהָם בִּבְחִינַת הַנְּקֻדָּה הַמְּאִירָה מִנֵּהּ וּבֵהּ, כִּי הָיָה יָחִיד בָּעוֹלָם בִּבְחִינַת אֶחָד הָיָה אַבְרָהָם, וְאַחַר כָּךְ כְּשֶׁזָּכָה לְהוֹלִיד אֶת יִצְחָק אָז הָיָה בְּחִינַת טוֹבִים הַשְּׁנַיִם וְכַנַּ"ל. וּכְשֶׁהוֹלִיד יִצְחָק אֶת יַעֲקֹב אָז הַחוּט הַמְשֻׁלָּשׁ לֹא בִּמְהֵרָה יִנָּתֵק, כִּי אָז נִשְׁלַם הַקְּדֻשָּׁה, כִּי אָז נִמְשְׁכָה אוֹר הַנְּקֻדָּה בְּכָל הַשָּׁלֹשׁ בְּחִינוֹת הַנַּ"ל. וְעַל-כֵּן יַעֲקֹב אָבִינוּ קִבֵּל הַנְּקֻדָּה בְּשָׁלֹשׁ בְּחִינוֹת הַנַּ"ל, כִּי קִבְּלָהּ מֵאֲבִי אָבִיו בִּבְחִינַת נְקֻדַּת הַזָּקֵן שֶׁהוּא הָרַב שֶׁבַּדּוֹר וְקִבְּלָה מֵאָבִיו בִּבְחִינַת נְקֻדַּת הַחֲבֵרִים כַּנַּ"ל וְקִבְּלָה מִנֵּהּ וּבֵהּ. וְעַל-כֵּן בֶּאֱמֶת לֹא נִשְׁלְמָה הַקְּדֻשָּׁה עַד יַעֲקֹב שֶׁהָיְתָה מִטָּתוֹ שְׁלֵמָה. אֲבָל אַבְרָהָם יָצָא מִמֶּנּוּ יִשְׁמָעֵאל וְיִצְחָק יָצָא מִמֶּנּוּ עֵשָׂו, כִּי אָז עֲדַיִן לֹא נִתְבַּטְּלָה הַחֶרְפָּה וְהַשְּׁבִירָה שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת עָרְלָה דְּחַפְיָא עַל בְּרִית. שֶׁעַל-יְדֵי-זֶה יָצָא מֵהֶם יִשְׁמָעֵאל וְעֵשָׂו שֶׁהֵם מִבְּחִינַת הַחֶרְפָּה וְהַשְּׁבִירָה מִבְּחִינַת מַלְכִין קַדְמָאִין דְּמִיתוּ שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת וְאֵלֶּה הַמְלָכִים אֲשֶׁר מָלְכוּ בְּאֶרֶץ אֱדוֹם לִפְנֵי מְלֹךְ מֶלֶךְ לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל, כַּיָּדוּעַ. וְכָל זֶה מֵחֲמַת שֶׁעֲדַיִן לֹא הֵאִירָה הַנְּקֻדָּה בִּשְׁלֵמוּת בְּכָל הַשָּׁלֹשׁ בְּחִינוֹת כַּנַּ"ל. שֶׁעַל-יְדֵי-זֶה לֹא נִתְבַּטְּלָה לְגַמְרֵי הַחֶרְפָּה וְהַשְּׁבִירָה שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת יִשְׁמָעֵאל וְעֵשָׂו, אֲבָל יַעֲקֹב שֶׁהָיָה חוּט הַמְשֻׁלָּשׁ. וְאָז נִשְׁלְמָה אוֹר הַנְּקֻדָּה בְּכָל הַשָּׁלֹשׁ בְּחִינוֹת כַּנַּ"ל. וְאָז נִתְבַּטֵּל הַחֶרְפָּה וְהַשְּׁבִירָה לְגַמְרֵי וְזָכָה לִשְׁמִירַת הַבְּרִית בְּתַכְלִית, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב, רֵאשִׁית אוֹנִי. עַל-כֵּן הָיְתָה מִטָּתוֹ שְׁלֵמָה בְּלִי שׁוּם פְּסוּל, כִּי אָז נִתְבַּטְּלָה הַחֶרְפָּה וְהַשְּׁבִירָה לְגַמְרֵי עַל-יְדֵי שֶׁנִּשְׁלְמָה אוֹר הַנְּקֻדָּה בְּכָל הַשָּׁלֹשׁ בְּחִינוֹת כַּנַּ"ל: וְזֶה שֶׁאָמְרוּ רַבּוֹתֵינוּ זַ"ל, כָּל מִי שֶׁהוּא תַּלְמִיד חָכָם וּבְנוֹ תַּלְמִיד חָכָם וּבֶן בְּנוֹ תַּלְמִיד חָכָם שׁוּב אֵין הַתּוֹרָה פּוֹסֶקֶת מִזַּרְעוֹ, שֶׁנֶּאֱמַר, וַאֲנִי וְכוּ' לֹא יָמוּשׁוּ מִפִּיךָ וּמִפִּי זַרְעֲךָ וּמִפִּי זֶרַע זַרְעֲךָ וְכוּ'. כִּי אָז מְאִירָה הַנְּקֻדָּה בְּכָל הַשָּׁלֹשׁ בְּחִינוֹת שֶׁעַל-יְדֵי-זֶה עִקַּר קִיּוּם הַתּוֹרָה כַּנַּ"ל:</div>

<p class="first">And therefore one must learn with his son and with his grandson, as it is written, <span class="verse-inline">"And you shall make them known to your sons and your sons' sons"</span> etc. — and not with them alone etc. — but one must give them precedence etc. For the Torah, which is the aspect of the light of the <em>nekudah</em>, must be received through <strong>three aspects</strong>: (1) to speak with the <em>tzadik</em>, to receive from him the all-inclusive <em>nekudah</em>; (2) to speak with one's fellow, in the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"and they receive one from another"</span>; (3) to speak between oneself and one's Creator, to receive the <em>nekudah</em> from within oneself (<em>minei u'vei</em>), as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And it is known that in <em>kedushah</em>, each and every aspect must be included within all the aspects. And therefore the <em>nekudah</em> of each individual Jew must illuminate through all three aforementioned aspects. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"And you shall make them known to your sons and your sons' sons"</span> — corresponding to the three <em>nekudos</em> mentioned above.</p>

<p>For when one learns by himself and speaks words of Torah with his mouth and binds them to his heart, then the <em>nekudah</em> illuminates from his mouth to his heart — from within himself (<em>minei u'vei</em>). And when he merits having a son, he is obligated to learn with him. And then, <span class="verse-inline">"two are better than one, for if they fall, the one will raise up his fellow"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Ecclesiastes 4:9-10)</span> — for then the <strong>second <em>nekudah</em></strong> illuminates: that each Jew illuminates in his fellow, in the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"and they receive one from another."</span> For sons are also called brothers and fellows, as it is written, <span class="verse-inline">"And Yaakov said to his brothers: gather stones"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Genesis 31:46)</span> — and Rashi explained: <span class="verse-inline">"'to his brothers' means to his sons, who were brothers approaching him"</span> etc.</p>

<p>And when he merits a grandson, he is obligated to learn with him. And then, <span class="verse-inline">"the threefold cord is not quickly broken"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Ecclesiastes 4:12)</span>. For then he must learn with his son and with his grandson, and then his <em>nekudah</em> is magnified and becomes the aspect of the <strong>light of the <em>nekudah</em> of the Rav</strong> — since he merits learning with two: with his son and with his grandson. For then his son himself learns with his son, who is his grandson, and their learning together is the aspect of the light of the <em>nekudah</em> of the friends, as mentioned above. And he becomes the <em>zakain</em> <span class="added">[elder]</span> and learns with both of them. Therefore his <em>nekudah</em> is in the aspect of the <em>nekudas haRav</em> — who is called <em>zakain</em>, <span class="verse-inline">"this one has acquired wisdom."</span> For he illuminates in both — in his son and grandson — in the aspect of the <em>nekudas haRav</em>, which is the aspect of the <em>zakain</em>. And his son and grandson illuminate in each other in the aspect of the <em>nekudas hachaveirim</em>. And each one learns by himself and illuminates the <em>nekudah</em> from within himself (<em>minei u'vei</em>). And then all three <em>nekudos</em> illuminate, and all the <em>charafos</em> and <em>sheviros</em> are nullified, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>For the three aforementioned <em>nekudos</em> are the aspect of the <strong>three <em>Avos</em>: Avraham, Yitzchak, and Yaakov</strong>. At first, Avraham's <em>nekudah</em> was only from within himself (<em>minei u'vei</em>) — for he was the first to recognize his Creator in completeness and began illuminating the light of the <em>nekudah</em> in the world, to nullify the <em>charafos</em> and <em>sheviros</em> that had intensified since the sin of Adam Harishon. For Avraham merited <em>kehunah</em>, the aspect of holy love, in the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"You are a <em>kohein</em> forever"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Psalms 110:4)</span>. And this he merited through striving all his days in <em>hisbodedus</em>, speaking between himself and his Creator and speaking with people — through which he merited illuminating the light of the <em>nekudah</em>. And through this he merited the holy love that rests near the <em>nekudah</em>, in the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"Avraham My beloved"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Isaiah 41:8)</span>, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>But at first Avraham's <em>nekudah</em> was in the aspect of the <em>nekudah</em> that illuminates from within itself — for he was alone in the world, in the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"Avraham was one"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Ezekiel 33:24)</span>. And afterwards, when he merited giving birth to Yitzchak, then it was the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"two are better,"</span> as mentioned above. And when Yitzchak gave birth to Yaakov, then <span class="verse-inline">"the threefold cord is not quickly broken"</span> — for then the <em>kedushah</em> was completed, for then the light of the <em>nekudah</em> was drawn through all three aforementioned aspects.</p>

<p>And therefore Yaakov Avinu received the <em>nekudah</em> through all three aforementioned aspects: he received it from the father of his father, in the aspect of the <em>nekudas hazakain</em>, who is the Rav of the generation; and he received it from his father, in the aspect of the <em>nekudas hachaveirim</em>, as mentioned above; and he received it from within himself (<em>minei u'vei</em>). And therefore in truth, the <em>kedushah</em> was not completed until Yaakov — whose bed was complete. But from Avraham came forth Yishmael, and from Yitzchak came forth Eisav — for then the <em>cherpah</em> and <em>sh'virah</em>, which are the aspect of the <em>orlah</em> that covers the <em>bris</em>, had not yet been nullified. Through which came forth from them Yishmael and Eisav, who are from the aspect of the <em>cherpah</em> and <em>sh'virah</em>, from the aspect of the <em>malchin kadma'in d'misu</em> <span class="added">[the primordial kings that died]</span> — which are the aspect of <span class="verse-inline">"And these are the kings who reigned in the land of Edom before a king reigned over the children of Israel"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Genesis 36:31)</span>, as is known.</p>

<p>And all this was because the <em>nekudah</em> had not yet illuminated in completeness through all three aforementioned aspects — through which the <em>cherpah</em> and <em>sh'virah</em>, which are the aspect of Yishmael and Eisav, had not been completely nullified. But Yaakov, who was the threefold cord — and then the light of the <em>nekudah</em> was completed through all three aspects, as mentioned above — then the <em>cherpah</em> and <em>sh'virah</em> were completely nullified, and he merited the guarding of the <em>bris</em> in its utmost, as it is written, <span class="verse-inline">"the first of my vigor (<em>raishis oni</em>)"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Genesis 49:3)</span>. Therefore his bed was complete, without any disqualification — for then the <em>cherpah</em> and <em>sh'virah</em> were completely nullified through the completion of the light of the <em>nekudah</em> through all three aspects, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="verse-inline">"Whoever is a <em>talmid chacham</em>, and his son is a <em>talmid chacham</em>, and his grandson is a <em>talmid chacham</em> — the Torah will never cease from his descendants, as it says, 'And I</span> etc. <span class="verse-inline">they shall not depart from your mouth, and from the mouth of your children, and from the mouth of your children's children'"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Isaiah 59:21)</span> etc. For then the <em>nekudah</em> illuminates through all three aspects — through which is the primary fulfillment of the Torah, as mentioned above.</p>

